# PART 2: CRITICAL EXISTING FIREBASE INTEGRATION

THIS SECTION HAS THE HIGHEST PRIORITY.

## 2.1 Existing Project Requirement

An existing Redcover Android application already uses Firebase.

The website MUST connect to that same existing Firebase project.

Do not create a new Firebase project.

Do not create a separate backend.

Do not replace the existing Firebase configuration.

Do not migrate existing users or books to another database.

Do not delete or overwrite existing data.

## 2.2 First Task: Inspect Existing Project

Before developing the website, inspect all available project files and determine:

1. Existing Firebase project ID.
2. Firebase configuration.
3. Authentication providers.
4. Firestore database configuration.
5. Existing collections.
6. Existing document structures.
7. User model.
8. Book model.
9. Author model.
10. Friendship and following models.
11. Chat and message models.
12. Notification models.
13. Membership model.
14. Reading progress model.
15. Review and rating model.
16. Admin authorization model.
17. Firebase Storage bucket.
18. Existing book cover storage paths.
19. Existing PDF storage paths.
20. Existing Firebase Security Rules.

If the Android source code is available, inspect its actual implementation instead of guessing how the backend works.

If the source code is unavailable, inspect the existing project configuration and request the necessary backend documentation or access.

Do not assume collection names.

Do not assume document field names.

Do not invent existing Firebase paths.

## 2.3 Firebase Services

Reuse the existing services wherever they are configured:

- Firebase Authentication.
- Cloud Firestore.
- Firebase Storage.
- Firebase Realtime Database, if the existing application uses it.
- Firebase Cloud Messaging, if configured.
- Cloud Functions, if configured.
- Firebase App Check, if configured.

Do not introduce a replacement service without explaining why it is necessary and receiving approval.

## 2.4 Shared Authentication

The same Redcover account must work across Android and Web.

Expected behavior:

1. A user registers through Android.
2. The user can log in through Redcover Web.
3. The website loads the existing user profile.
4. The user can access existing books and saved items.
5. Changes made on the website appear in Android wherever the existing data model supports synchronization.
6. Changes made in Android appear on the website.

Reuse the existing authentication providers.

Implement appropriate web authentication persistence.

Handle:
- Login.
- Registration, if supported.
- Logout.
- Forgot password.
- Authentication state restoration.
- Session expiration.
- Unauthorized access.
- Email verification, if configured.

Do not create a second user account for an existing user.

Important: Inspect provider configuration and Firebase authorized domains before implementing web authentication.

## 2.5 Database Compatibility Layer

Create a modular data-access layer.

Suggested architecture:

src/
  services/
    firebase/
      firebaseConfig.ts
      authService.ts
      databaseService.ts
      storageService.ts
    repositories/
      userRepository.ts
      bookRepository.ts
      socialRepository.ts
      chatRepository.ts
      notificationRepository.ts
      membershipRepository.ts
      readingRepository.ts

The repository layer must adapt the existing Firebase schema to reusable frontend models.

Do not change the underlying database schema simply to make the frontend easier to build.

If existing Android field names differ from the website's preferred frontend names, create mapping functions.

Example conceptual frontend models:

User:
- uid
- username
- displayName
- email
- photoURL
- coverURL
- bio
- followersCount
- followingCount
- membership
- createdAt

Book:
- id
- title
- description
- authorId
- authorName
- coverURL
- fileURL
- category
- language
- rating
- views
- likes
- publishedAt
- status

These are conceptual examples only.

The actual implementation must follow the existing Firebase schema.

## 2.6 Firebase Storage

Use the existing Redcover Firebase Storage bucket.

Book covers and uploaded book images must use Firebase Storage.

Do NOT use Cloudinary.

Do not duplicate existing book images.

Reuse existing storage paths and download URLs wherever possible.

Implement:
- Upload progress.
- File validation.
- File size validation.
- Upload cancellation where practical.
- Upload failure recovery.
- Secure file access.
- Image optimization.
- PDF access permissions.

Never expose service account credentials or privileged Firebase secrets in frontend code.

## 2.7 Data Synchronization

The website and Android application must remain compatible.

Examples:

- Updating a profile on Web updates the same user document.
- Saving a book on Web updates the same saved-book relationship.
- Reading progress is stored in the compatible existing format.
- Following a user uses the existing relationship structure.
- Publishing a book makes it visible to Android where the existing application supports it.
- Notifications use the existing notification architecture.

Do not create duplicate books, users, or social relationships.

Use transactions or batched writes when multiple related database operations need consistency.

## 2.8 Firebase Security

Security is mandatory.

Implement:
- Authentication checks.
- Ownership validation.
- Role-based access control.
- Admin authorization.
- Firestore Security Rules compatibility.
- Storage Security Rules compatibility.
- Input validation.
- File upload validation.
- Protection against unauthorized modifications.

Never trust a role value stored only in frontend state.

Verify administrator authorization using trusted backend-controlled data.

Do not loosen existing Firebase Security Rules just to make the website work.

If a rule change is necessary, propose the exact change and explain its effect before deployment.

