# PART 9: READING EXPERIENCE

This section must receive special attention.

The reader should be comfortable for long reading sessions.

## 9.1 Reader Interface

Create a distraction-free interface.

Top toolbar:
- Back.
- Book title.
- Chapter title.
- Font controls.
- Theme controls.
- Bookmark.
- Reading settings.

Main area:
- Centered reading column.
- Comfortable line length.
- Good typography.
- Proper paragraph spacing.

Bottom:
- Previous Chapter.
- Next Chapter.
- Progress indicator.

## 9.2 Reading Modes

Support:
- Light Mode.
- Dark Mode.
- Sepia Mode, if practical.

## 9.3 Typography Controls

Allow:
- Increase font size.
- Decrease font size.
- Change line spacing.
- Change reading width where practical.

Persist user preferences locally or through the existing compatible profile settings.

## 9.4 Reading Progress

Automatically save:
- Book ID.
- Current chapter.
- Reading position.
- Percentage.
- Last reading time.

Use debounced updates or appropriate batching to avoid excessive database writes.

Do not save progress on every keystroke or scroll event.

Use a sensible saving interval and save on meaningful navigation events.

## 9.5 Bookmarks and Highlights

Users should be able to:
- Bookmark a reading position.
- Add highlights where supported.
- Add private notes.
- Reopen saved reading positions.

Store these using the existing data structures if available.

## 9.6 PDF Reading

Inspect the existing Android PDF reading implementation.

Determine how PDF files are stored and how access is authorized.

Implement a compatible web PDF viewer.

Requirements:
- Page navigation.
- Zoom.
- Fit width.
- Progress saving.
- Full-screen mode.
- Download controls only when permitted.

Do not expose private PDFs through public URLs.

Do not assume all books are PDFs.

